{"id":"[REDACTED]","object":"chat.completion","created":1790984962,"model":"mercury-2","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"**Answer**\n\nThe only remaining, reachable evidence‑loss mechanism introduced by the new `codex‑challenger` change is the **discarding of non‑object frames and SSE events during parsing**.  \n\n* `parseCodexTapes` reads the stdout stream line‑by‑line, JSON‑parses each line and **only pushes the result into `frames` when it is a plain object** (`if (frame && typeof frame === 'object' && !Array.isArray(frame)) frames.push(frame)`). All other lines are recorded as parse errors (`parseErrors.push('invalid_exec_frame')` or `'invalid_exec_jsonl'`) and the raw line is **not kept in any structured field**【open_file†L31-L40】.  \n\n* The same logic is applied to the stderr SSE stream: only objects are stored in `events`; everything else becomes a parse error (`parseErrors.push('invalid_provider_event')` or `'invalid_provider_sse'`) and is not retained in a structured list【open_file†L42-L51】.  \n\n* After parsing, the client stores **only the parsed events** in `metadata.providerFrames` (`providerFrames: parsed.events`) and the raw stdout/stderr buffers in `metadata.rawResponse` / `metadata.rawError`【open_file†L160-L161】.  \n\nConsequences  \n\n* Down‑stream code that relies on `metadata.providerFrames` (e.g., `classifyFableFallbackError` in `adversarial-review.js`) sees **only the successfully parsed events**. Any non‑object event that was dropped is invisible to those consumers, even though the raw text is still present in `rawResponse`.  \n\n* The loss is **reachable** because the `providerFrames` field is the canonical source for identity‑posture and tool‑call analysis; missing events can change the outcome of those analyses without any further warning beyond the generic `parseErrors` list.  \n\nThus, while the change records explicit parse errors and preserves the raw tapes, it still **silently discards the content of malformed frames/events from the structured `providerFrames` view**, which constitutes an evidence‑loss path for any consumer that does not inspect the raw buffers.\n\n---\n\n**Self‑report footer**\n\n- **WHAT I DID:** Opened `codex‑challenger.js`, examined `parseCodexTapes` and the metadata construction, and traced the usage of `providerFrames` in downstream code.  \n- **WHAT I DID NOT DO:** Execute any runtime code, modify files, or search beyond the relevant parsing and metadata sections.  \n- **WHAT I ASSUMED:** The repository state at commit `7072895a8cac96ca9fd0f9d1ad6ab56356fe8117` is the current source; no hidden or generated files alter the parsing logic.  \n- **WHY THIS VERDICT:** The parsing functions explicitly drop non‑object frames/events (citations) and the downstream consumers rely on `metadata.providerFrames` (implicit via `classifyFableFallbackError`). This creates a concrete, reachable evidence‑loss mechanism.  \n- **IF INCOMPLETE, WHY:** Not applicable – the evidence is directly observable in the source.","tool_calls":[],"tool_call_id":null},"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":21634,"completion_tokens":769,"total_tokens":22403,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":3886},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":122}},"warning":null,"reasoning_summary":null}